  "strategy": "Approach and rationale\nWe will use a machine learning-inspired optimization method called \"Neural Network Guidance.\" This approach combines a random initialization with a refinement phase that mimics the pruning and regularization steps seen in training deep learning models. The core idea is to start with a sequence of uniform values (normalized to sum = sqrt(2n)), then iteratively adjust the sequence to reduce the maximum convolution value while keeping the sum within plausible bounds. This is achieved by:\n1. Selecting an extremum index from the convolution result (mode 'full')\n2. Optimizing a small \"sub-sequence\" neighborhood around that index\n3. Comparing the result with an original subgradient optimization to ensure real progress\n\nWe use an exponentially increasing sequence length (from 128 to 2048) to ensure diversity, but we also include a multi-stage refinement approach to escape local minima. Unlike the previous program, we avoid solving LP problems directly and instead use gradient-based optimization with special handling for discontinuities.\n\nMathematical specification\nWe consider a sequence a \u2208 \u211d^n with sum(a) \u2265 0.01 and non-negative elements capped at 1000. The optimization focuses on minimizing:\nscore = 2*n*max(convolve(a, mode='full')) / (sum(a))^2\n\nWe use stochastic gradient descent with exponential learning rate decay, constrained by:\n- Non-negativity\n- Maximum element scaling\n- A smoothing penalty term to avoid sharp discontinuities\n\nImplementation procedure\n1. Initialize `a` with cosinusoidal taper (to avoid initial bad solutions) for length n\n2. Precompute full convolution `b = np.convolve(a, mode='full')`\n3. For each iteration:\n   - Find the index `k` with highest `b[k]`\n   - Define a sub-sequence window `window` around `k`\n   - Set a new gradient function to minimize `convolve` in this window\n   - Use exponential backtracking linesearch with projected gradients\n   - Apply stochastic gradients to maximize deviation from uniform distribution while reducing max convolve\n4. Store the best scored sequence found during the process\n5. After 1800 seconds, return the best found sequence\n\nSearch choices\nThe algorithm uses dynamic window selection based on the following:\n- Window size is 3*initial_index_spacing (initially 10% of the absolute value of the index)\n- The length n starts exponentially increasing from 128 to 2048\n- Gradient direction is computed from the full convolution and gradient descent step\n- We incorporate a smoothing parameter (alpha) to fight discontinuities\n\nThe candidate sequence is initialized as a vector passing a simplex projection and normalization.\n\nComputational budget\n- We allow up to 1800 seconds for the entire process\n- Exponential length increase: 128, 768, 960, 1024, 1228, 2048 (6 lengths)\n- For each length, we run a fixed number of optimization iterations (300)\n- Each iteration uses a small window and gradient descent with careful time accounting\n\nValidation and acceptance contract\nThe final candidate is evaluated by normalizing (making sum=1) and computing the raw score. We track the smallest score found during the optimization process. We ensure strict continuity with the initial evaluation function.\n\nThe sequence is provided as a NumPy array of floats (initialized to uniform taper) and undergoes iterative improvement without synthetic operations. We prepare to return the best found candidate for the longest length that had improved results.\n\nPseudocode:\n1. Define the exponential length progression (128 to 2048)\n2. Initialize sequence with cosine taper\n3. Compute initial score\n4. Set best_score to that initial evaluation\n5. For each length with score < threshold (1.5027):\n   a. Set current best sequence as the global optimum so far\n   b. Set learning_rate = 0.02 / k (k=iterations)\n   c. While time is available:\n      - Find extremum index from full convolve\n      - Define sub-sequence window [-10, 10] around k+initial sum offset\n      - Use gradient descent to find improved values in that window\n      - Update only the window coefficients with the gradient\n   d. Track the best valid candidate (normalized to sum=1)\n6. Choose the candidate with smallest score from all considered lengths\n\nWe must be cautious with dimension indexes and variable types to prevent unintended capping.\n\nThis method provides genuine theoretical improvements by focusing on the peak reaction and using a refined neighborhood optimization.",
